#### Writing a Function Handler (Dart runtime)

```dart
// lib/main.dart — Appwrite Function entry point
Future<dynamic> main(final context) async {
    // context.req.body        — raw body (String)
    // context.req.bodyJson    — parsed JSON (Map or null)
    // context.req.headers     — headers (Map)
    // context.req.method      — HTTP method
    // context.req.path        — URL path
    // context.req.query       — query params (Map)

    context.log('Processing: ${context.req.method} ${context.req.path}');

    if (context.req.method == 'GET') {
        return context.res.json({'message': 'Hello from Appwrite Function!'});
    }

    return context.res.json({'success': true});      // JSON
    // return context.res.text('Hello');              // plain text
    // return context.res.empty();                    // 204
    // return context.res.redirect('https://...');    // 302
}
```

### Server-Side Rendering (SSR) Authentication

SSR apps using server-side Dart (Dart Frog, Shelf, etc.) use the **server SDK** (`dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}`) to handle auth. You need two clients:

- **Admin client** — uses an API key, creates sessions, bypasses rate limits (reusable singleton)
- **Session client** — uses a session cookie, acts on behalf of a user (create per-request, never share)

```dart
import 'package:dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}/dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}.dart';

// Admin client (reusable)
final adminClient = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setKey(Platform.environment['{{ spec.info.title | caseUpper }}_API_KEY']!);

// Session client (create per-request)
final sessionClient = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]');

final session = request.cookies['a_session_[PROJECT_ID]'];
if (session != null) {
    sessionClient.setSession(session);
}
```

#### Email/Password Login

```dart
final account = Account(adminClient);
final session = await account.createEmailPasswordSession(
    email: body['email'],
    password: body['password'],
);

// Cookie name must be a_session_<PROJECT_ID>
response.headers.add('Set-Cookie',
    'a_session_[PROJECT_ID]=${session.secret}; '
    'HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict; '
    'Expires=${HttpDate.format(DateTime.parse(session.expire))}; Path=/');
```

#### Authenticated Requests

```dart
final session = request.cookies['a_session_[PROJECT_ID]'];
if (session == null) {
    return Response(statusCode: 401, body: 'Unauthorized');
}

final sessionClient = Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .setSession(session);

final account = Account(sessionClient);
final user = await account.get();
```

#### OAuth2 SSR Flow

```dart
// Step 1: Redirect to OAuth provider
final account = Account(adminClient);
final redirectUrl = await account.createOAuth2Token(
    provider: OAuthProvider.github,
    success: 'https://example.com/oauth/success',
    failure: 'https://example.com/oauth/failure',
);
return Response(statusCode: 302, headers: {'Location': redirectUrl});

// Step 2: Handle callback — exchange token for session
final account = Account(adminClient);
final session = await account.createSession(
    userId: request.uri.queryParameters['userId']!,
    secret: request.uri.queryParameters['secret']!,
);
// Set session cookie as above
```

> **Cookie security:** Always use `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and `SameSite=Strict` to prevent XSS. The cookie name must be `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>`.

> **Forwarding user agent:** Call `sessionClient.setForwardedUserAgent(request.headers['user-agent'])` to record the end-user's browser info for debugging and security.

## Error Handling

```dart
import 'package:{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}/{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}.dart';
// AppwriteException is included in the main import

try {
    final row = await tablesDB.getRow(databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]', tableId: '[TABLE_ID]', rowId: '[ROW_ID]');
} on AppwriteException catch (e) {
    print(e.message);    // human-readable message
    print(e.code);       // HTTP status code (int)
    print(e.type);       // error type (e.g. 'document_not_found')
    print(e.response);   // full response body (Map)
}
```

**Common error codes:**

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `401` | Unauthorized — missing or invalid session/API key |
| `403` | Forbidden — insufficient permissions |
| `404` | Not found — resource does not exist |
| `409` | Conflict — duplicate ID or unique constraint |
| `429` | Rate limited — too many requests |

## Permissions & Roles (Critical)

Appwrite uses permission strings to control access to resources. Each permission pairs an action (`read`, `update`, `delete`, `create`, or `write` which grants create + update + delete) with a role target. By default, **no user has access** unless permissions are explicitly set at the row/file level or inherited from the table/bucket settings. Permissions are arrays of strings built with the `Permission` and `Role` helpers.

```dart
import 'package:{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}/{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}.dart';
// Permission and Role are included in the main package import
```

### Database Row with Permissions

```dart
final doc = await tablesDB.createRow(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: '[TABLE_ID]',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {'title': 'Hello World'},
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),     // specific user can read
        Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),   // specific user can update
        Permission.read(Role.team('[TEAM_ID]')),     // all team members can read
        Permission.read(Role.any()),                 // anyone (including guests) can read
    ],
);
```

### File Upload with Permissions

```dart
final file = await storage.createFile(
    bucketId: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    fileId: ID.unique(),
    file: InputFile.fromPath(path: '/path/to/file.png', filename: 'file.png'),
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any()),
        Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
        Permission.delete(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
    ],
);
```

> **When to set permissions:** Set row/file-level permissions when you need per-resource access control. If all rows in a table share the same rules, configure permissions at the table/bucket level and leave row permissions empty.

> **Common mistakes:**
> - **Forgetting permissions** — the resource becomes inaccessible to all users (including the creator)
> - **`Role.any()` with `write`/`update`/`delete`** — allows any user, including unauthenticated guests, to modify or remove the resource
> - **`Permission.read(Role.any())` on sensitive data** — makes the resource publicly readable

## Multi-tenancy with Teams

Model each tenant (an organization, workspace, school, or customer account) as a **team**. Memberships carry the tenant roles, and `Role.team()` permissions keep one tenant's rows and files invisible to every other tenant. All tenants share one project and one set of tables.

| Tenant concept | {{ spec.info.title }} primitive |
|----------------|---------------------|
| Tenant | Team (the team ID is the tenant ID) |
| User belongs to a tenant | Membership (`confirm: true` once joined) |
| Tenant roles (`owner`, `admin`, `member`) | Membership `roles` (free-form strings) |
| Tenant-only data | Row/file permissions with `Role.team(tenantId)` or `Role.team(tenantId, 'admin')` |
| Tenant settings (plan, branding) | Team prefs (`teams.getPrefs` / `teams.updatePrefs`). Every member can read them, so never store secrets there |
| Platform staff across all tenants | A user label set server-side with `users.updateLabels`, granted through `Role.label('staff')` |

### Configure Tenant Tables (server-side, once)

Turn on row security and keep table-level permissions to `create`. Table-level permissions apply to **every row**, and a user needs access at the table level *or* the row level, so `Permission.read(Role.users())` on the table lets every signed-in user read every tenant's rows.

```dart
import 'package:dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}/dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}.dart';
import 'package:dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}/enums.dart' as enums;
import 'package:dart_{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}/models.dart' as models;

final tablesDB = TablesDB(adminClient); // API key client

await tablesDB.createTable(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'invoices',
    name: 'Invoices',
    rowSecurity: true,                               // row permissions decide who sees each row
    permissions: [Permission.create(Role.users())],  // signed-in users may create rows; nothing else table-wide
    columns: [
        {'key': 'tenantId', 'type': 'varchar', 'size': 36,  'required': true},
        {'key': 'title',    'type': 'varchar', 'size': 255, 'required': true},
        {'key': 'amount',   'type': 'double',               'required': true},
    ],
);

// Index tenantId so per-tenant filters stay fast (the column must be available first)
await tablesDB.createIndex(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'invoices',
    key: 'tenant_idx',
    type: enums.TablesDBIndexType.key,
    columns: ['tenantId'],
);
```

If only server code creates rows, leave the table-level `permissions` empty.

### Create a Tenant

Client-side (Flutter), the signed-in user creates the team and becomes its owner:

```dart
final teams = Teams(client);

final tenant = await teams.create(
    teamId: ID.unique(),
    name: 'Example Corp',
    roles: ['owner', 'admin'],   // the CREATOR's roles (default: ['owner'])
);
```

> **`roles` on `teams.create()` sets the creator's roles.** It does not declare which roles the team supports. A role exists as soon as a membership carries it. Roles are flat strings: `owner` does not imply `admin`. Only `owner` is special: owners invite and remove members, change roles, update the team, and delete it.

Server-side, `teams.create()` with an API key creates a team with **no members**. Add the owner yourself:

```dart
final adminTeams = Teams(adminClient);

final tenant = await adminTeams.create(teamId: ID.unique(), name: 'Example Corp');
await adminTeams.createMembership(
    teamId: tenant.$id,
    roles: ['owner', 'admin'],
    userId: '[USER_ID]',         // with an API key the user joins at once; no invite email
);
```

### Invite and Manage Members

A client-side invite sends an email and creates a pending membership (`confirm: false`) until the user accepts. Only owners can invite.

```dart
await teams.createMembership(
    teamId: '[TENANT_ID]',
    roles: ['member'],
    email: 'user@example.com',
    url: 'https://example.com/accept-invite',   // hostname must be registered as a platform
);
```

{{ spec.info.title }} appends `teamId`, `membershipId`, `userId`, and `secret` to `url`. Accept from the page or deep link that `url` opens. A successful accept also creates a session for the invited user.

```dart
// uri is the incoming link (from app_links, go_router, or Uri.base on Flutter web)
await teams.updateMembershipStatus(
    teamId: uri.queryParameters['teamId']!,
    membershipId: uri.queryParameters['membershipId']!,
    userId: uri.queryParameters['userId']!,
    secret: uri.queryParameters['secret']!,
);
```

With an API key, `createMembership()` sends no email and adds the user immediately. Use it for SSO provisioning, bulk imports, or a custom invite flow.

```dart
// Tenants the signed-in user belongs to (client-side), for a tenant switcher
final myTenants = (await teams.list()).teams;

// Members of a tenant (any member can list them)
final members = await teams.listMemberships(teamId: '[TENANT_ID]');

// The current user's roles in a tenant. Use them for UI decisions only; permissions do the enforcing
final me = await account.get();
final mine = await teams.listMemberships(
    teamId: '[TENANT_ID]',
    queries: [Query.equal('userId', me.$id)],
);
final isAdmin = mine.memberships.isNotEmpty && mine.memberships.first.roles.contains('admin');

// Change a member's roles (owners only)
await teams.updateMembership(
    teamId: '[TENANT_ID]',
    membershipId: '[MEMBERSHIP_ID]',
    roles: ['admin'],
);

// Remove a member, revoke a pending invite, or leave the tenant (users can delete their own membership)
await teams.deleteMembership(teamId: '[TENANT_ID]', membershipId: '[MEMBERSHIP_ID]');
```

Deleting a membership revokes access at once: the user stops matching every `Role.team()` permission for that tenant. To hide member names, emails, and MFA status from other members, enable membership privacy in the Console under **Auth** > **Policies** > **Memberships**.

### Write and Read Tenant Data

```dart
// Tag every row with its tenant and grant access through the tenant's team
await tablesDB.createRow(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'invoices',
    rowId: ID.unique(),
    data: {'tenantId': '[TENANT_ID]', 'title': 'Q2 Invoice', 'amount': 2500},
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]')),             // every member of the tenant
        Permission.update(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]', 'admin')),  // tenant admins only
        Permission.delete(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]', 'admin')),
    ],
);

// Returns only rows the user can read, from ALL of the user's tenants.
// Filter by tenantId to show the active tenant.
final invoices = await tablesDB.listRows(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'invoices',
    queries: [Query.equal('tenantId', '[TENANT_ID]')],
);
```

> **Client SDKs can only grant roles the user holds.** A user whose only role is `member` gets `401` when setting `Permission.update(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]', 'admin'))`. From the client, grant roles the user has (such as `Role.team('[TENANT_ID]')`), or create the row in a server function, where the API key can grant any role.

Files follow the same pattern: create the bucket with `fileSecurity: true` and give each file `Role.team()` permissions. Realtime needs no extra work: subscribers only receive events for rows and files they can read.

### Server Code: API Keys Ignore Tenant Boundaries

An API key bypasses all permissions, so an admin client sees every tenant's data. For requests made on behalf of a user:

1. **Act as the user** with the per-request session client from the SSR section, or with a JWT. Permissions then isolate tenants for you.
2. **If you must use the API key**, confirm the caller's membership and roles first, then filter every query by `tenantId`. Take the user ID from the verified session or the `x-appwrite-user-id` function header, never from the request body.

```dart
// Appwrite Function: act as the calling user
final jwt = context.req.headers['x-appwrite-user-jwt'];   // present only when a signed-in user called the function
if (jwt == null) throw Exception('Unauthorized');           // or return an error response

final userClient = Client()
    .setEndpoint(Platform.environment['APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT']!)
    .setProject(Platform.environment['APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID']!)
    .setJWT(jwt);

final invoices = await TablesDB(userClient).listRows(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'invoices',
    queries: [Query.equal('tenantId', '[TENANT_ID]')],
);

// API key path: verify membership before touching tenant data
Future<models.Membership> requireTenantRole(String tenantId, String userId, [String? role]) async {
    final list = await Teams(adminClient).listMemberships(
        teamId: tenantId,
        queries: [Query.equal('userId', userId)],
    );
    final confirmed = list.memberships.where((m) => m.confirm);
    if (confirmed.isEmpty || (role != null && !confirmed.first.roles.contains(role))) {
        throw Exception('Forbidden');
    }
    return confirmed.first;
}
```

### Offboard a Tenant

Deleting a team removes its memberships, not the rows and files that grant it access, and a team created later with the same ID would inherit them. Delete tenant data first, server-side, in every tenant table and bucket:

```dart
await tablesDB.deleteRows(
    databaseId: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    tableId: 'invoices',
    queries: [Query.equal('tenantId', '[TENANT_ID]')],
);

await adminTeams.delete(teamId: '[TENANT_ID]');
```

### Choosing an Isolation Model

| Model | Use when | Trade-off |
|-------|----------|-----------|
| Shared tables + team permissions (default) | Most SaaS apps | One schema for all tenants; isolation depends on correct permissions |
| Table or database per tenant | Tenants need their own schema, or their data must be exported or dropped as a unit | Every schema change runs once per tenant. Put `Role.team(tenantId)` in the table-level permissions and leave row security off |
| Project per tenant | Tenants need separate users, auth settings, or regions | Users and sessions cannot cross projects; far more to operate |

> **Common mistakes:**
> - **Table-level `read`/`update`/`delete` for `Role.users()` or `Role.any()` on a tenant table**: exposes every tenant's rows
> - **Row security left off**: row permissions are ignored and only table-level permissions count
> - **Treating `tenantId` as the security boundary**: it is a filter, and clients can write any value into it. Permissions are the boundary
> - **Serving user requests with the API key and no membership check**: the key sees every tenant
> - **Granting a role the client user lacks**: fails with `401`; grant it from server code instead
> - **Expecting `owner` to imply `admin`**: roles are flat strings; give users every role your permissions check
> - **Deleting a team but not its data**: orphaned rows remain, and a future team with the same ID inherits them

